From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:38:28 -0400
Subject: feat: Stedding updates itself on Windows, with a version of its own

The Windows half of the updater (docs/features/updates.md Up14 to Up22;
ADR 0023, and ADR 0026 for its key), and the version it needs (BACKLOG S-72):

- Stedding's own version on Windows. tooling/win/build.ps1 writes the
  product's version into chrome/VERSION before each build:
  <milestone>.<100 x major + minor>.<patch>.<beta n, 999 for a release>, so
  0.2.0-beta.19 on Chromium 156 is 156.2.0.19 and each release installs
  above the one before it on one Chromium pin, as an in-use update rather
  than a repair. Chromium's own version rides on a CHROMIUM_VERSION line,
  which version_info::GetChromiumVersionNumber() returns, and everything the
  web and Google's services see keeps it: the user agent, its client hints
  and navigator.userAgentData, the Web Store's and the component updater's
  prodversion, extensions' minimum Chrome version, the Web Store's
  getFullChromeVersion, the version sent to Safe Browsing, GCM, sync,
  OAuth and with a Google image search, the DIAL search's user agent, the
  root store's version rules, and About's Chromium line. Without the line,
  as on the Mac, both versions are the same.
- stedding::AppUpdater on Windows (app_updater_win.cc), started from
  ChromeBrowserMainPartsWin::PostProfileInit, and only in a copy the
  installer put in place: ten seconds after launch when a day has passed,
  then daily, under the Mac's "Update Stedding automatically", it reads
  updates/appcast-windows.xml with no cookies and no cache, checks the
  feed's Ed25519 signature (Sparkle's signed-feed comment) against the key
  every Windows build carries, parses it in the sandboxed XML parser
  (update_feed.cc), downloads a later Windows installer, checks its length
  and signature off the UI thread, and runs it hidden. One check runs at a
  time; the feed request ends after a minute and the download after two
  hours; the download's folder is made off the UI thread and its name is
  checked; a quit does not wait for a running install, and the
  installer's folder is deleted before the quit ends. The installer is
  checked and run on one handle no one may write to meanwhile; a signed feed
  older than one already read is refused as a replay; plain http to this
  computer is allowed only to a test feed; a check that cannot reach the
  feed tries again within the hour; automatic updates turned off during a
  download install nothing; a failed install's log is kept; old download
  folders are cleared at launch (BACKLOG S-249). Chromium's in-use
  update puts the new version beside the running one; the sidebar's pill
  then offers the restart, which is Chromium's own (chrome::AttemptRestart
  swaps new_chrome.exe in). An update installed and not yet restarted into
  is offered at launch. --stedding-update-feed points a copy at a test feed,
  still checked against the same key.
- The uninstaller deletes the PDF handler's ProgId key as well, which
  upstream leaves behind (BACKLOG S-71).
- About on Windows reads the same updater as the Mac's
  (version_updater_stedding.cc, renamed from _mac), and Settings shows the
  switch on Windows too.

Tests: UpdateFeedTest, UpdateFeedItemsTest and WinAppUpdaterTest (unit),
ChromiumVersionTest.PagesAndServicesSeeChromiumsVersion (browser), and the
n-1 check on the PC (docs/HANDOFF.md, "The updater (Windows)").

Why: the owner asked for the Windows version to update itself as the Mac's
does (2026-10-19); a Chromium fork that cannot deliver its own security
updates leaves its users behind, and an update must install above the
version running, which Chromium's number alone cannot give two releases on
one pin.
Removable when: never as a whole; a move to another mechanism (Chromium's
updater, an MSIX package) replaces the updater half, and the version half
stays while Stedding ships more than one release on a Chromium build.
